From cecb18a8ea4045fe76ca783034c20f8f9c2b3d57 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chandler Newman <chandler2newman@hotmail.co.uk>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 08:34:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] vhost-user: serve SHMEM_MAP and SHMEM_UNMAP backend requests

A vhost-user device may publish shared memory regions to the guest, each
appearing as part of a PCI BAR. The backend owns what is in them: it asks the
frontend to place a file descriptor at an offset inside a region, and to take
it away again. virtio-fs uses this for a DAX cache, virtio-gpu for
host-visible blob resources.

The message types have been available since vhost 0.16.0 and nothing consumed
them. The scaffolding was already here -- both virtio-fs and the generic
device carry a cache field, expose it through get_shm_regions, and the generic
device already negotiates BACKEND_REQ -- but no device could serve a request
to map into one, so no cache was ever configured.

ShmemRegions resolves a request against the windows a device published and
places the descriptor with MAP_FIXED. The guest has the whole window mapped
already, so memory placed there becomes visible without any further change to
its address space. Removing a mapping leaves the range absent rather than
keeping whatever was last there, so a guest reading a stale offset faults
instead of seeing another resource memory. Offsets and lengths come from the
backend, which is a separate and not necessarily trusted process, so a request
that overflows or leaves the window is refused rather than passed to mmap.

The shared memory capability also carries an identifier the guest driver looks
a region up by, and the specification makes it a property of the device rather
than of the region position in a list. It was derived from the list index,
which is correct only because virtio-fs uses 0 and is the sole consumer.
virtio-gpu uses 1 for its host-visible window, so the identifier now travels
with the region.

--generic-vhost-user gains shm_size and shm_id so a window can be configured.
